VALUES (
  'wrt_2e02a18c5119b9aae23d139e73490160','usr_ffbc49c36dc84f0889dfb5de2f68ccc3','pieces-fragments-of-myself','Pieces','Personal essay from high school (AP Lit final project, 2022)','essay','public','public',
  'I invite you to understand me through the fragments of myself, though they are, just as I am, a piece of me.','{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 1}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pieces"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "High-school final project essay — a fragmentary personal narrative written alongside Virginia Woolf’s Mrs Dalloway, about identity, memory, and becoming.", "marks": [{"type": "italic"}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Introduction"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "August (ambiguous date), 2021"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I wish I can describe to you exactly how beautiful my world is: every frustration and headache, every laughter, every sorrow. I wish I can tell you how it is in my life, and I wish that you’d understand, but I’m sure that you don’t. If only I can give you my eyes, my heart, my brain, my everything, then maybe a part of me will stay with you, and you’d only then be able to see a slice of what it takes to be me. The rest I’m still trying to figure out myself."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I was born on June 9th, 2004 at some time in the afternoon. In every past version of myself, something echoes a fate that can only be chosen by myself. In every book I have read, every character I became, I became a little bit more myself. Everything I read and write is a fragment of myself. Every decision that I made and shall ever make transcends into me, and it is the thought of this that grants me comfort- the thought that I am every fragment of me. I invite you to understand me through the fragments of myself, though they are, just as I am, a piece of me, and though I will never truly know who I will be, what I want to be."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 1: Birth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "My mother said I was an obnoxious toddler. I cried and cried, refused to eat what was fed to me, and rebelled against the world in the few ways my infancy allowed. I don’t remember my infancy, but I do remember that I cried often. Perhaps it ended when I first entered elementary school, or maybe the day when I first cried about a math test. My tears were never-ending, though those were the best years of my life."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "March 2nd, 2022 9:51 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Childhood"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I murdered my childhood in her sleep"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Wrapped my arms around her until silence"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Planted my palms across her mouth until mute"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "She died in my arms"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I was her casket"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I sold her ashes to Youth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Smothered her with the lasting ache of guilt"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That she killed her childhood and not me"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 2: Youth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "October 8th, 2018 7:28 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Belong"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Perhaps I do"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But one could argue"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Because I don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "At least not immediately"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Maybe I’ll grow up one day"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "And then my world would be better"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But one could argue"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Because I can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "At least not immediately"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I do not recall what life was like day to day before I moved to the U.S. All I recall now are the mundane, the little things that made me happy. I had a childhood friend who was three years older than I, we read stories together. She is now a sophomore in college, and I never had a friend as close as her. Decidedly, I chose to be lonely. In time, socializing grew daunting, and I wrapped myself in the cacoon. I fixated upon myself and my education. I forgot what life was like when I cried whenever I wanted."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "“And there is a dignity in people; a solitude; even between husband and wife a gulf; and that one must respect, thought Clarissa, watching him open the door; for one would not part with it oneself, or take it, against his will, from one’s husband, without losing one’s independence, one’s self-respect—something, after all, priceless.”"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "— Virginia Woolf, Mrs. Dalloway (pg 120)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Mrs. Dalloway said she would buy the flowers herself."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Between each of her acquaintances and her was the distance of their thoughts, that drive them apart yet brings them relief. Each character in Dalloway lives in the world of their thoughts, as I live with mine. From Dalloway to Boxfish to Eyre, I feel the power of independence in their distance, from the world, from themselves, but not from their goals. The flowers were a symbol of her identity, and she needed to go alone, saying that she would be alone. As a teenager I tried to emulate the characters I read in books. I still do, decidedly, and never know what part of my character belongs to me. In the story of Dalloway, I saw a calmness that I do not have. Though I always liked to be alone, I dreaded loneliness equally. I was not Dalloway nor Boxfish or Eyre, but their stories inspire me to find the goals I would never stray away from. Dalloway’s phrases have a conclusiveness that comes with age, a decisiveness on what she wants and believes in, and that to me is the self-respect I wish to have in the turbulent world of my thoughts."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "October 1st, 2019 10:38 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Hi, it’s me again. I am a sophomore now; fifteen. Fifteen was never perfect. Perhaps, life has never been perfect, alluding to the millions of quotes that I can easily cite for the imperfection of the world, both in the past, the present, or the future; your choice. If you might not remember, your fifteenth year of chaos began with perhaps the most chaotic birthday ever yet. There was no birthday cake or presents or friends…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I dislike many things. I am melancholic by nature and dissatisfied with many choices of mine, and perhaps that was why infant me cried on and on. I miss the summer still, but I often mean the summer that I spent at my grandparents’ house when I was six. I am very attached to my history that I often fear the future and how my family and my past self might dislike me in the present, might dislike me from the future. I could never put my thoughts down, never calm or controlled internally."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 3: Adulthood"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "November 8th, 2018 9:41 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Some days I want to be a Homosapien"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Instead of a human"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Homosapiens are just any animal"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Humans aren’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We are held accountable for things many animals don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In charge of many things animals can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Some days that’s a good thing"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But today I just want to be a little Homosapien"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Small enough to not be noticed"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Large enough to not be stepped on"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That is my ultimate goal in life."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I haven''t answered the prompt yet, about my future. My birthday lands exactly one month and four days from the moment I put this line down, and I would be an adult. Oftentimes I focus too much on the past that I do not like to think of the future. As a child I believed that I could do anything, only to be told I can’t. Though now people try to inspire a dream out of me that I do not have. Decidedly, I dislike this project because I cannot find a way to make an argument about what I want for my future when I don’t have one. Neither can I say, in so many words, that I can’t, without explaining my whole life story. Like Richard Dalloway, I am the epitome of hesitance. I do not stress about who I want to be, though this phrase is a partial lie. I worry about how the world perceives me, but I hide from the world. Should I change? I ask myself, often. Yet often I question my self-doubt, my motives for change, and my goals, and I never change. I write the same poems I wrote five years ago, though with more fluency in rhyme. My goal in life is to simply exist, though that is also a lie. I wish to be calmer and stop overthinking, I tell myself. Perhaps that should be my birthday wish this year. They never come true anyway."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Prayer"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Whatever happens. Whatever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "what is is is what"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I want. Only that. But that."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "—Galway Kinnell"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In the words of Kinnell, “Whatever what is is is what I want”. This poem caught my eye the second I saw it. Short poems often do, for they seem to express an air of disinterest to impress the wide majority. Oftentimes I fear showing people my writing in case my words come across in ways I do not intend. For the past week, I have been trying to choose parts of my writing that are less fake, less pretentious, and less pessimistic. This poem reminded me that the more profound things often come in very simple lessons. In the three-lined poem of precisely fourteen words, the repetition is immaculate- four words contain “what”, along with three “is” in a row. Kinnell’s poem, though seemingly a very profound answer to what he wants in life, appears more like a rhetorical question, perhaps the reason for the title, “Prayer”. Though the message feels decisive, the diction choices invoke ambiguity, and “but that” refers to a change in thought immediately after the first thought. I identify with the thought of ambiguity and fear for knowledge. I fear that I’d become a cruel adult, an unloving partner, a despondent elder. I’d like to fit in, but fear who I might become. I have a fear of missing out, though often not of fun, but of all the different lives I could have lived by living in my head."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "September 17th, 2021 9:47 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The other day I dreamt of a day in a life, where I had friends, where I was productive, and where I was so happy and loved. The details are beyond me now, but for once in my life, I did not fear interactions, I enjoyed what my life is without anxiety or stress, and I had a cat. A white-furred, majestic feline, graceful and non-provoking, waiting for me as I returned home one day, tired yet happy from my day''s worth of exertion. I extended my arms, though hands full of stuff and in such a heavy down jacket and sweating lightly in it. For a moment I paused in fear, that the soul I loved might turn away, might claw me from objection, or dislike my embrace of it, for certainly, I would be happier to remain boundaries as it would please. In my second of hesitation, the cat came forth, and placed its little head on my chest, the fur rubbing against my neck. It was perhaps the happiest dream I’ve ever had, of a life I might never have."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Monet Refuses the Operation (excerpt)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I will not return to a universe"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of objects that don''t know each other,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "as if islands were not the lost children"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of one great continent. The world"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "is flux, and light becomes what it touches,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "becomes water, lilies on water,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "above and below water,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "becomes lilac and mauve and yellow"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and white and cerulean lamps,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "small fists passing sunlight"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "so quickly to one another"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "that it would take long, streaming hair"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "inside my brush to catch it."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "To paint the speed of light!"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Our weighted shapes, these verticals,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "burn to mix with air"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and changes our bones, skin, clothes"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to gases. Doctor,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "if only you could see"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "how heaven pulls earth into its arms"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and how infinitely the heart expands"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to claim this world, blue vapor without end."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "—Lisel Mueller"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "When I look at my life I sometimes refuse the concept of change, for fear nothing would be the same. Though I often wish to be more straightforward with my thoughts and stop the endless stream of anxiety, I fear that everything about this me would be unreal. In Lisel Mueller’s poem, when Monet faces the operation to remove cataracts, he speaks of all the ways that might alter his understanding of the world, all the sights that might disappear if his vision grew clearer. He spoke of painting “the speed of light… weighted shapes…”, the abstract, the unwhole, the personally valuable parts of his artistic identity. To him, if his vision was clear, if the lines were redrawn in his world, he loses the part of him who sees islands as “the lost children of one great continent”- the romantic and emotional vision. Using metaphors and symbolism to compare the real world to the wonderful sights he sees, Monet presents a fear of change in his artistic visions. Though I do not see great sights from islands, I too worry that I lose myself and the world I know if I stop worrying."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter ℵ0: The Permanent Death"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "March 5th, 2018 10:00AM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "when leaves fall"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "no one would remember"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "they have no value"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "they fall in September"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "but in the end"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who would remember"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "we are just memories"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "due in December"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In December it ends"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "in December they’re gone"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "the leaves live again"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "but we won’t be found"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "August (ambiguous date), 2021"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Sometimes I wonder what death feels like, if death feels anything. To lie in peace with not thought and not feeling, to gradually ease out of existence painlessly, to have no sensation of pain or suffering. I imagine that it might be like slipping into deep coma, though rather than a dream, I’m met with a dark abysm of nothingness. Blind, deaf, senseless, meaningless. As a child I dreaded the concept of death, to lay in the darkness of a grave and bear an eternity of a state not proven to be inpermanent. And people will forget me, and I shall be erased from the face of the world…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[W]hen Evans was killed, just before the Armistice, in Italy, Septimus, far from showing any emotion of recognising that here was the end of a friendship, congratulated himself on feeling very little and very reasonably. The War had taught him. It was sublime."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "— Virginia Woolf, Mrs. Dalloway (pg 86)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "My great-grandmother died when I was seven years old. I was right by her side when it happened. The time between her last two breaths lasted eight seconds, and then she stopped breathing for all of eternity. I didn’t quite understand what death was when it happened, but now I realize that the sight is forever imprinted in my memories. Septimus Warren Smith didn’t understand death until he joined the war. He was a young poet who wanted to be noble until the war took away his agency and identity. Upon leaving, he didn’t feel sad, though not because he felt reasonably, but because the part of him that would be sad had died in the war. The irony in the war being “sublime” sticks only when we witness Septimus’s fits of madness. Rationally, we all know death the be the solution to all our problems this life, we tend to question our birth and the suffering that comes with our life. Though we all long for conclusiveness, we don''t know what is on the other side of death. Most of us choose to not change because it is worthwhile, some of us choose to not change for fear of death. “To be or not to be?” Ultimately we don’t have a choice in what happens to us in life, if we ever needed one for anything."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "February 2nd, 2022 21:43 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Bibliography"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can write a bibliography"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of me, for you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a tale of infancy till future"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "tell you everything, every"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "secret, buried"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "emotions, hurried"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "wishes. Wish I could tell you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who am I in simple words."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here’s the thing."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "It is easy to be ignorant,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "self-deprecating, self-aggrandizing, selfish"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to say I have it figured out,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "when I chose the default"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to not choose, to not care, to not"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "be present in the world."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I could tell you lies and not know"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can tell you truth and change sides"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can’t tell you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who am I in simple words"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Before I end I would like to apologize to whoever had to read this. It is unbearably long, yet this is the closest I can come to an argument about my life. When I was a toddler I wanted to be famous, when I was a pre-teen I wanted to be smart, when I was a teenager I wanted to fit in. I still want to be all of those things, but I mostly want to stop thinking about what I want now. I don’t really look forward to college, or dating someone, or getting a job, but I look forward to watching a movie next weekend, existing, and wearing my new shoes."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Perhaps that is enough. Only that. But that."}]}]}',NULL,1785481355305,1785481355305,1785481355305,NULL,0,NULL
);
INSERT INTO writing_versions (id, writing_id, structured_content_snapshot, title_snapshot, source, created_at)
VALUES ('wver_9e3a19888a0223cafea5e286','wrt_2e02a18c5119b9aae23d139e73490160','{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 1}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Pieces"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "High-school final project essay — a fragmentary personal narrative written alongside Virginia Woolf’s Mrs Dalloway, about identity, memory, and becoming.", "marks": [{"type": "italic"}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Introduction"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "August (ambiguous date), 2021"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I wish I can describe to you exactly how beautiful my world is: every frustration and headache, every laughter, every sorrow. I wish I can tell you how it is in my life, and I wish that you’d understand, but I’m sure that you don’t. If only I can give you my eyes, my heart, my brain, my everything, then maybe a part of me will stay with you, and you’d only then be able to see a slice of what it takes to be me. The rest I’m still trying to figure out myself."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I was born on June 9th, 2004 at some time in the afternoon. In every past version of myself, something echoes a fate that can only be chosen by myself. In every book I have read, every character I became, I became a little bit more myself. Everything I read and write is a fragment of myself. Every decision that I made and shall ever make transcends into me, and it is the thought of this that grants me comfort- the thought that I am every fragment of me. I invite you to understand me through the fragments of myself, though they are, just as I am, a piece of me, and though I will never truly know who I will be, what I want to be."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 1: Birth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "My mother said I was an obnoxious toddler. I cried and cried, refused to eat what was fed to me, and rebelled against the world in the few ways my infancy allowed. I don’t remember my infancy, but I do remember that I cried often. Perhaps it ended when I first entered elementary school, or maybe the day when I first cried about a math test. My tears were never-ending, though those were the best years of my life."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "March 2nd, 2022 9:51 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Childhood"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I murdered my childhood in her sleep"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Wrapped my arms around her until silence"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Planted my palms across her mouth until mute"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "She died in my arms"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I was her casket"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I sold her ashes to Youth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Smothered her with the lasting ache of guilt"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That she killed her childhood and not me"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 2: Youth"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "October 8th, 2018 7:28 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Belong"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Perhaps I do"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But one could argue"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Because I don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "At least not immediately"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Maybe I’ll grow up one day"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "And then my world would be better"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But one could argue"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Because I can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "At least not immediately"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I do not recall what life was like day to day before I moved to the U.S. All I recall now are the mundane, the little things that made me happy. I had a childhood friend who was three years older than I, we read stories together. She is now a sophomore in college, and I never had a friend as close as her. Decidedly, I chose to be lonely. In time, socializing grew daunting, and I wrapped myself in the cacoon. I fixated upon myself and my education. I forgot what life was like when I cried whenever I wanted."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "“And there is a dignity in people; a solitude; even between husband and wife a gulf; and that one must respect, thought Clarissa, watching him open the door; for one would not part with it oneself, or take it, against his will, from one’s husband, without losing one’s independence, one’s self-respect—something, after all, priceless.”"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "— Virginia Woolf, Mrs. Dalloway (pg 120)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Mrs. Dalloway said she would buy the flowers herself."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Between each of her acquaintances and her was the distance of their thoughts, that drive them apart yet brings them relief. Each character in Dalloway lives in the world of their thoughts, as I live with mine. From Dalloway to Boxfish to Eyre, I feel the power of independence in their distance, from the world, from themselves, but not from their goals. The flowers were a symbol of her identity, and she needed to go alone, saying that she would be alone. As a teenager I tried to emulate the characters I read in books. I still do, decidedly, and never know what part of my character belongs to me. In the story of Dalloway, I saw a calmness that I do not have. Though I always liked to be alone, I dreaded loneliness equally. I was not Dalloway nor Boxfish or Eyre, but their stories inspire me to find the goals I would never stray away from. Dalloway’s phrases have a conclusiveness that comes with age, a decisiveness on what she wants and believes in, and that to me is the self-respect I wish to have in the turbulent world of my thoughts."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "October 1st, 2019 10:38 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Hi, it’s me again. I am a sophomore now; fifteen. Fifteen was never perfect. Perhaps, life has never been perfect, alluding to the millions of quotes that I can easily cite for the imperfection of the world, both in the past, the present, or the future; your choice. If you might not remember, your fifteenth year of chaos began with perhaps the most chaotic birthday ever yet. There was no birthday cake or presents or friends…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I dislike many things. I am melancholic by nature and dissatisfied with many choices of mine, and perhaps that was why infant me cried on and on. I miss the summer still, but I often mean the summer that I spent at my grandparents’ house when I was six. I am very attached to my history that I often fear the future and how my family and my past self might dislike me in the present, might dislike me from the future. I could never put my thoughts down, never calm or controlled internally."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter 3: Adulthood"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "November 8th, 2018 9:41 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Some days I want to be a Homosapien"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Instead of a human"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Homosapiens are just any animal"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Humans aren’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We are held accountable for things many animals don’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In charge of many things animals can’t"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Some days that’s a good thing"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "But today I just want to be a little Homosapien"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Small enough to not be noticed"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Large enough to not be stepped on"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That is my ultimate goal in life."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I haven''t answered the prompt yet, about my future. My birthday lands exactly one month and four days from the moment I put this line down, and I would be an adult. Oftentimes I focus too much on the past that I do not like to think of the future. As a child I believed that I could do anything, only to be told I can’t. Though now people try to inspire a dream out of me that I do not have. Decidedly, I dislike this project because I cannot find a way to make an argument about what I want for my future when I don’t have one. Neither can I say, in so many words, that I can’t, without explaining my whole life story. Like Richard Dalloway, I am the epitome of hesitance. I do not stress about who I want to be, though this phrase is a partial lie. I worry about how the world perceives me, but I hide from the world. Should I change? I ask myself, often. Yet often I question my self-doubt, my motives for change, and my goals, and I never change. I write the same poems I wrote five years ago, though with more fluency in rhyme. My goal in life is to simply exist, though that is also a lie. I wish to be calmer and stop overthinking, I tell myself. Perhaps that should be my birthday wish this year. They never come true anyway."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Prayer"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Whatever happens. Whatever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "what is is is what"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I want. Only that. But that."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "—Galway Kinnell"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In the words of Kinnell, “Whatever what is is is what I want”. This poem caught my eye the second I saw it. Short poems often do, for they seem to express an air of disinterest to impress the wide majority. Oftentimes I fear showing people my writing in case my words come across in ways I do not intend. For the past week, I have been trying to choose parts of my writing that are less fake, less pretentious, and less pessimistic. This poem reminded me that the more profound things often come in very simple lessons. In the three-lined poem of precisely fourteen words, the repetition is immaculate- four words contain “what”, along with three “is” in a row. Kinnell’s poem, though seemingly a very profound answer to what he wants in life, appears more like a rhetorical question, perhaps the reason for the title, “Prayer”. Though the message feels decisive, the diction choices invoke ambiguity, and “but that” refers to a change in thought immediately after the first thought. I identify with the thought of ambiguity and fear for knowledge. I fear that I’d become a cruel adult, an unloving partner, a despondent elder. I’d like to fit in, but fear who I might become. I have a fear of missing out, though often not of fun, but of all the different lives I could have lived by living in my head."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "September 17th, 2021 9:47 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The other day I dreamt of a day in a life, where I had friends, where I was productive, and where I was so happy and loved. The details are beyond me now, but for once in my life, I did not fear interactions, I enjoyed what my life is without anxiety or stress, and I had a cat. A white-furred, majestic feline, graceful and non-provoking, waiting for me as I returned home one day, tired yet happy from my day''s worth of exertion. I extended my arms, though hands full of stuff and in such a heavy down jacket and sweating lightly in it. For a moment I paused in fear, that the soul I loved might turn away, might claw me from objection, or dislike my embrace of it, for certainly, I would be happier to remain boundaries as it would please. In my second of hesitation, the cat came forth, and placed its little head on my chest, the fur rubbing against my neck. It was perhaps the happiest dream I’ve ever had, of a life I might never have."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Monet Refuses the Operation (excerpt)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I will not return to a universe"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of objects that don''t know each other,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "as if islands were not the lost children"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of one great continent. The world"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "is flux, and light becomes what it touches,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "becomes water, lilies on water,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "above and below water,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "becomes lilac and mauve and yellow"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and white and cerulean lamps,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "small fists passing sunlight"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "so quickly to one another"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "that it would take long, streaming hair"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "inside my brush to catch it."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "To paint the speed of light!"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Our weighted shapes, these verticals,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "burn to mix with air"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and changes our bones, skin, clothes"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to gases. Doctor,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "if only you could see"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "how heaven pulls earth into its arms"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "and how infinitely the heart expands"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to claim this world, blue vapor without end."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "—Lisel Mueller"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "When I look at my life I sometimes refuse the concept of change, for fear nothing would be the same. Though I often wish to be more straightforward with my thoughts and stop the endless stream of anxiety, I fear that everything about this me would be unreal. In Lisel Mueller’s poem, when Monet faces the operation to remove cataracts, he speaks of all the ways that might alter his understanding of the world, all the sights that might disappear if his vision grew clearer. He spoke of painting “the speed of light… weighted shapes…”, the abstract, the unwhole, the personally valuable parts of his artistic identity. To him, if his vision was clear, if the lines were redrawn in his world, he loses the part of him who sees islands as “the lost children of one great continent”- the romantic and emotional vision. Using metaphors and symbolism to compare the real world to the wonderful sights he sees, Monet presents a fear of change in his artistic visions. Though I do not see great sights from islands, I too worry that I lose myself and the world I know if I stop worrying."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Chapter ℵ0: The Permanent Death"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "March 5th, 2018 10:00AM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "when leaves fall"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "no one would remember"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "they have no value"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "they fall in September"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "but in the end"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who would remember"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "we are just memories"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "due in December"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In December it ends"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "in December they’re gone"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "the leaves live again"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "but we won’t be found"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "August (ambiguous date), 2021"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Sometimes I wonder what death feels like, if death feels anything. To lie in peace with not thought and not feeling, to gradually ease out of existence painlessly, to have no sensation of pain or suffering. I imagine that it might be like slipping into deep coma, though rather than a dream, I’m met with a dark abysm of nothingness. Blind, deaf, senseless, meaningless. As a child I dreaded the concept of death, to lay in the darkness of a grave and bear an eternity of a state not proven to be inpermanent. And people will forget me, and I shall be erased from the face of the world…"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[W]hen Evans was killed, just before the Armistice, in Italy, Septimus, far from showing any emotion of recognising that here was the end of a friendship, congratulated himself on feeling very little and very reasonably. The War had taught him. It was sublime."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "— Virginia Woolf, Mrs. Dalloway (pg 86)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "My great-grandmother died when I was seven years old. I was right by her side when it happened. The time between her last two breaths lasted eight seconds, and then she stopped breathing for all of eternity. I didn’t quite understand what death was when it happened, but now I realize that the sight is forever imprinted in my memories. Septimus Warren Smith didn’t understand death until he joined the war. He was a young poet who wanted to be noble until the war took away his agency and identity. Upon leaving, he didn’t feel sad, though not because he felt reasonably, but because the part of him that would be sad had died in the war. The irony in the war being “sublime” sticks only when we witness Septimus’s fits of madness. Rationally, we all know death the be the solution to all our problems this life, we tend to question our birth and the suffering that comes with our life. Though we all long for conclusiveness, we don''t know what is on the other side of death. Most of us choose to not change because it is worthwhile, some of us choose to not change for fear of death. “To be or not to be?” Ultimately we don’t have a choice in what happens to us in life, if we ever needed one for anything."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "February 2nd, 2022 21:43 PM"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Bibliography"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can write a bibliography"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "of me, for you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a tale of infancy till future"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "tell you everything, every"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "secret, buried"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "emotions, hurried"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "wishes. Wish I could tell you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who am I in simple words."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here’s the thing."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "It is easy to be ignorant,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "self-deprecating, self-aggrandizing, selfish"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to say I have it figured out,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "when I chose the default"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "to not choose, to not care, to not"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "be present in the world."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I could tell you lies and not know"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can tell you truth and change sides"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can’t tell you"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "who am I in simple words"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Before I end I would like to apologize to whoever had to read this. It is unbearably long, yet this is the closest I can come to an argument about my life. When I was a toddler I wanted to be famous, when I was a pre-teen I wanted to be smart, when I was a teenager I wanted to fit in. I still want to be all of those things, but I mostly want to stop thinking about what I want now. I don’t really look forward to college, or dating someone, or getting a job, but I look forward to watching a movie next weekend, existing, and wearing my new shoes."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Perhaps that is enough. Only that. But that."}]}]}','Pieces','seed',1785481355305);
DELETE FROM writing_pieces WHERE slug = 'ode-to-life-ghazal';
INSERT INTO writing_pieces
(id, owner_id, slug, title, subtitle, type, status, visibility, excerpt,
 structured_content, cover_image_id, created_at, updated_at, published_at,
 archived_at, show_origin, source_conversation_id)
VALUES (
  'wrt_f496a7ebd91302cd9f43f6c6f421ccb6','usr_ffbc49c36dc84f0889dfb5de2f68ccc3','ode-to-life-ghazal','Ode to Life','Original ghazal and reflection from high school','poem','public','public',
  'And the soft song plays in the ears of all, that the phantom sound is clear.','{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 1}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Ode to Life"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "High-school original ghazal (with a short craft reflection) for AP Literature — radif: “forever.”", "marks": [{"type": "italic"}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "“I love that word. Forever. I love that forever doesn''t exist, but we have a word for it anyway, and use it all the time. It''s beautiful and doomed.”- Viv Albertine"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Ode to Life"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Hear: the soft song plays in the ears of all, that the phantom sound is clear. Like the golden-crowned"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "sparrow in the depth of fall, the melody rings in heavenly halls, the song: Forever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "And God looked upon us with a pitying glance, for he had forever to spare, while men"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "toiled through their purposeless endeavor, never aware of his Godly thought: Forever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In our depth of agony, our souls can only sense- the doom of the scent so ominous,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "the smell of death is near, his marching song, his mare. He charges on. Is death not Forever?"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We lie in the coalbed of destiny, blind idiots we are, ripped into living, teared"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "apart, glued together, gifted pain and glory, is that the solemn song of Forever?"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We collect jubilations to better our existence. We withdraw from pain, perpetual"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "resistance. Revolutionaries against destiny, and the terrors of Forever."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "If my name ‘joy’, meant anything at all, perhaps it will bring me certainty that I shall"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "live in the tune of the golden-crowned sparrow, not tomorrow, but certainly forever."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Reflection:"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The inspiration for the ghazal came from the quote by british singer and writer Viv albertine, which beautifully summarizes the mysterious concept of foreverness in contrast to the very spontaneous and always changing nature of life. It is strange that we often use the word forever without in fact meaning for it to represent what it actually is; it is a concept that we cannot grasp due to the perpetuity of the duration. As such, I chose my radif to be the word “forever”. I intended the poem to be a contemplation of the concept as an almost divine and otherworldly one, a song quite unable to be comprehended, though it is everywhere and in everything. In the maqta, I chose to refer to myself by the meaning of my Chinese name as a way to reference my own identity with the concept of forever. While the thought comes to me, I wave them away so as to live in the moment and not be swept away, ending with the contradicting message that I would live forever in the music of the golden-crowned sparrow as it is my own interpretation of the word forever. The first few couplets mention forever as a godly concept, thereby being capitalized, and when it reaches the last couplet, with my own interpretation, it is no longer capitalized."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Reflection"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The premise of the poem was inspired after watching a sad movie and crying a whole lot, when I thought it would be curious to have a conversation with an otherworldly spirit as an in-the-moment writing prompt. The prompt mostly became the first and second couplets. The initial premise of my poem was much shorter in syllables and much more enjambed, so to give myself more space to write, I decided to increase the syllable count to 22 syllables each line, which also gave me more space to play with rhyme and enjambment inside the couplets. As a more stylistic preference, I chose to add rhyme inside the couplets to break up the systematic format of the meter that I tend to see in most ghazals, while still following the basic rules of the poetic form. It is meant as a punctum, as most listeners tend not to find the radif and feel like something is a little off until they hear up to the second or third couplet. The number 22 was also a stylistic choice since the number 2 is often associated with balance and harmony, and to have 22 syllables is meant to hint to the otherworldly nature of the concept of foreverness. It also represents the duality hinted at in the poem by referencing both God and Death as the singers of the song of Forever. There were no major changes between the first draft and the final draft other than to change up a few words here and there so as to make each couplet more distinct and separate ideas. I tend to prefer to start my poems with transition phrases such as “like, and, but”, so to lean closer to the separation of couplets of the ghazal poetry form, I changed a few of those lines to allow them to be more distinct. I believed that I was able to successfully convey what I wished to in the first draft, so no other changes were made."}]}]}',NULL,1785481355305,1785481355305,1785481355305,NULL,0,NULL
);
INSERT INTO writing_versions (id, writing_id, structured_content_snapshot, title_snapshot, source, created_at)
VALUES ('wver_1ea7e78059282392dfe567c2','wrt_f496a7ebd91302cd9f43f6c6f421ccb6','{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 1}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Ode to Life"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "High-school original ghazal (with a short craft reflection) for AP Literature — radif: “forever.”", "marks": [{"type": "italic"}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "“I love that word. Forever. I love that forever doesn''t exist, but we have a word for it anyway, and use it all the time. It''s beautiful and doomed.”- Viv Albertine"}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Ode to Life"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Hear: the soft song plays in the ears of all, that the phantom sound is clear. Like the golden-crowned"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "sparrow in the depth of fall, the melody rings in heavenly halls, the song: Forever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "And God looked upon us with a pitying glance, for he had forever to spare, while men"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "toiled through their purposeless endeavor, never aware of his Godly thought: Forever"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In our depth of agony, our souls can only sense- the doom of the scent so ominous,"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "the smell of death is near, his marching song, his mare. He charges on. Is death not Forever?"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We lie in the coalbed of destiny, blind idiots we are, ripped into living, teared"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "apart, glued together, gifted pain and glory, is that the solemn song of Forever?"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We collect jubilations to better our existence. We withdraw from pain, perpetual"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "resistance. Revolutionaries against destiny, and the terrors of Forever."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "If my name ‘joy’, meant anything at all, perhaps it will bring me certainty that I shall"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "live in the tune of the golden-crowned sparrow, not tomorrow, but certainly forever."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Reflection:"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The inspiration for the ghazal came from the quote by british singer and writer Viv albertine, which beautifully summarizes the mysterious concept of foreverness in contrast to the very spontaneous and always changing nature of life. It is strange that we often use the word forever without in fact meaning for it to represent what it actually is; it is a concept that we cannot grasp due to the perpetuity of the duration. As such, I chose my radif to be the word “forever”. I intended the poem to be a contemplation of the concept as an almost divine and otherworldly one, a song quite unable to be comprehended, though it is everywhere and in everything. In the maqta, I chose to refer to myself by the meaning of my Chinese name as a way to reference my own identity with the concept of forever. While the thought comes to me, I wave them away so as to live in the moment and not be swept away, ending with the contradicting message that I would live forever in the music of the golden-crowned sparrow as it is my own interpretation of the word forever. The first few couplets mention forever as a godly concept, thereby being capitalized, and when it reaches the last couplet, with my own interpretation, it is no longer capitalized."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Reflection"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The premise of the poem was inspired after watching a sad movie and crying a whole lot, when I thought it would be curious to have a conversation with an otherworldly spirit as an in-the-moment writing prompt. The prompt mostly became the first and second couplets. The initial premise of my poem was much shorter in syllables and much more enjambed, so to give myself more space to write, I decided to increase the syllable count to 22 syllables each line, which also gave me more space to play with rhyme and enjambment inside the couplets. As a more stylistic preference, I chose to add rhyme inside the couplets to break up the systematic format of the meter that I tend to see in most ghazals, while still following the basic rules of the poetic form. It is meant as a punctum, as most listeners tend not to find the radif and feel like something is a little off until they hear up to the second or third couplet. The number 22 was also a stylistic choice since the number 2 is often associated with balance and harmony, and to have 22 syllables is meant to hint to the otherworldly nature of the concept of foreverness. It also represents the duality hinted at in the poem by referencing both God and Death as the singers of the song of Forever. There were no major changes between the first draft and the final draft other than to change up a few words here and there so as to make each couplet more distinct and separate ideas. I tend to prefer to start my poems with transition phrases such as “like, and, but”, so to lean closer to the separation of couplets of the ghazal poetry form, I changed a few of those lines to allow them to be more distinct. I believed that I was able to successfully convey what I wished to in the first draft, so no other changes were made."}]}]}','Ode to Life','seed',1785481355305);
