VALUES ('wver_e7899a361ada897c62b4b959','wrt_3015cb6cf3f4f7fe0f33fd94d956a163','{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 1}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Cynical Idealism"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "High-school AP Literature hybrid essay responding to Lillian Boxfish Takes a Walk — personal reflection woven with close reading on hope, cynicism, and endurance.", "marks": [{"type": "italic"}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"level": 2}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Cynical Idealism"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Ever since a certain day at age eleven, I have contemplated the question- is the human race inherently good or bad? It was a debate tournament amongst college students that later came to define my ideology, though at that moment it had simply piqued my interest to know that the winning side argued that it was bad, a resolution quite unlike what I had imagined. When I asked my dad for his position, his answer was somewhere along the lines of inherent evilness, and then laughed a little at the cynicism of his response. He paused for a moment, thinking a little, and gave me a peculiar answer- humans are hopeful, but the reality is disappointing. While I cannot remember his exact words now, the implications lived with me through the years, and as I grew up, I learned the struggle of having both hopefulness and realism, or in my words- cynical idealism, a hopefulness dulled by the real world, but a cynicism too optimistic about the future."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I toy with the thought often, the juxtaposition of hope and hopelessness, and upon walking with Lillian Boxfish through her journey on New Year''s Eve, I was reminded of the oxymoronic concept of a cynical idealist. As a cynical idealist herself, Lillian Boxfish portrays a conflicted ideology morphed through the reality of sexism and city violence but also the hopes of interpersonal connection and love. Kathleen Rooney argues for a need for hope and gratitude in an often disappointing reality through utilizing characterization and her metaphoric approach of painting Lillian’s life as a circle, ending Boxfish’s conflict with herself with the same metaphor as the beginning."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Throughout the book, Rooney paints Boxfish as a character that often appears undecided to readers as to the way that she approaches her life. While certain in her resolve to never marry, she falls in love faithfully, and while certain in her hate of certain things, seems to love others quite similar to those that she hates. However, the reason behind her characterization does not appear until the readers dive further into the book. Her struggles with her career, mental health, and sexism bring out the full Boxfish as a hopeful realist relentlessly continuing in her journey despite the struggles that she faces. Upon her journey on New Year''s Eve, Boxfish revisits Delmonico’s to find a friendly family willing to ask her to sit with them. In their conversation, she describes, “the constant low-grade lunacy of life in the city--but I am surprised to find, and I think they are too, that our stories emphasize the serendipitous, even the magical. Our tone is that of conspirators, as though we are afraid to be overheard speaking fondly of a city that conventional wisdom declares beyond hope”(153). Here, Rooney uses the juxtaposition of diction choices to magnify both the “lunacy” and the “serendipitous” that is her life in the city. While their conversation begins with the negative, it gradually changes into a hopeful tone for a better future, a drastic change in connotation Boxfish herself seems to be equally surprised about. While she compares their conversation to conspiracies, a gesture at the commonly accepted realism, Rooney paints an upbeat mood to reflect the value of hopefulness