iest, unwavering dream all along, and hopefulness for a future, no matter how short. By utilizing this form of structural callback, Rooney communicates the value of being gratuitous and hopeful through the worst of it all, because in the end, we would still come full circle in our journey in life, and that no matter what you do in life, it will all have been worth the struggle. Rooney glorifies the resolution, and the ups despite the downs, bringing Boxfish’s life full circle with a positive ending. With this artistic touch, she presents Boxfish’s entire life as an argument for remaining hopeful and gratuitous, making an example out of Boxfish’s seemingly downward spiral of life, but her remaining hope for her future. She makes a lesson out of the cynical idealism of Boxfish, and in turn inspires the readers to be hopeful during the lows of their lives."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Closing the book upon finishing the pages, I was hit face first with the implications that the adored cynical idealist represented in my life, and the debate that first introduced me to the concept of cynical idealism. While the concept had always existed, I had struggled to develop it further before I had encountered the complex characterization of Lillian Boxfish. I had always known myself to be cynically ideal, to marvel at kindness and sigh in disappointment at the world, and to dig myself out of emotional holes that I keep getting myself into. I recalled specifically the spring of my junior year when I was drowning in the stress that I put on myself while studying for my four AP tests. For almost two months I lacked sound sleep or a motivation to be joyous in school, repercussions of a mental burden too imminent to think clearly. I begged for the school year to end every day, and cried myself to sleep every other day. On the nights before each of my AP tests, I failed to sleep until four in the morning, when the birds had already begun to chirp and